WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.600
67 years. Three dozen movies. The&nbsp;
"Godzilla" franchise has had some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.800
of the highest highs and lowest&nbsp;
lows in monster cinema history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.360
Keep watching to know which movies to&nbsp;
include in your next "Godzilla" marathon.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:20.400
The worst of the worst is undoubtedly "Godzilla" –&nbsp;
not the original, but the American-made mess that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.960
somehow made it into theaters in 1998. Director&nbsp;
Roland Emmerich's take on the classic kaiju tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.760
really hard to do some cool stuff with a unique&nbsp;
story and modern special effects, but laughable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.520
CGI prevented it from having the punch producers&nbsp;
had hoped for. This version of Godzilla is barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.720
seen as it hides among buildings in New York —&nbsp;
because if there's anything Godzilla is known for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:47.280
it's hiding. That, and squeezing&nbsp;
through subway tunnels for some reason.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.040
The closest thing the film has to a&nbsp;
saving grace is Matthew Broderick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.600
but even Ferris Bueller himself can't save&nbsp;
this movie from being a complete blunder.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:02.240
Most fans would agree that the 1998 "Godzilla" has&nbsp;
had a negative impact on the franchise as a whole.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:07.840
"Aw damn, uh, that is a negative impact,&nbsp;
I repeat, that is a negative impact."

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.520
They gave it their best shot, but Netflix&nbsp;
definitely missed the mark when they put out their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:19.040
run of animated Godzilla movies. "Godzilla: Planet&nbsp;
of the Monsters," "Godzilla: City on the Edge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.760
Battle," and "Godzilla: The Planet Eater" aren't&nbsp;
as outrageously out-of-place in the Godzilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.520
canon as the 1998 film, but they're certainly not&nbsp;
quite outstanding enough to earn individual spots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.960
on this list. This trilogy tries desperately&nbsp;
to compensate for a lack of compelling story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.840
with vibrant animation, but the drama just isn't&nbsp;
there thanks to a lack of memorable characters.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.520
This series gives a look at a post-apocalyptic&nbsp;
future Earth where humans have traveled into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:51.520
space to escape Godzilla and other monsters'&nbsp;
continuous destruction. The concept is compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.040
and could have made for a fantastic&nbsp;
new take on the Godzilla mythos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.760
but the execution doesn't&nbsp;
exactly stick the landing.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:04.000
"Godzilla vs. Megaguirus" is another example of a&nbsp;
film that attempted to do something unique within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.000
the Godzilla universe, but came out lukewarm at&nbsp;
best. Unlike the hulking beasts that Godzilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.360
is typically known for fighting, the Meganulons&nbsp;
featured in this entry in the Millennium series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:21.440
are more like a swarm of annoying mosquitos. It's&nbsp;
only at the end of the movie, when Godzilla faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.400
off with the Meganulon queen Megaguirus,&nbsp;
that things actually start to get exciting.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.960
The plot is mediocre at best,&nbsp;
reworking the Millennium-era Godzilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.560
into its own being while somehow&nbsp;
managing to stay disconnected from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:40.320
every film that came before it. Add that to the&nbsp;
infamously bad acting from the human characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.680
and you get one of many misguided attempts&nbsp;
to further the franchise after the Y2K scare.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.840
Most viewers would agree that "Godzilla's&nbsp;
Revenge," also known as "All Monsters Attack,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.120
isn't the worst of films, but it's a tedious&nbsp;
watch and among the most skippable entries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.640
in the franchise. "Godzilla's Revenge" was&nbsp;
geared toward small children in an attempt to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:09.040
find new demographics for a declining franchise.&nbsp;
Unsurprisingly, this didn't sit well with adults.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:14.320
The film is from the perspective of a bullied&nbsp;
child watching poorly cut-together stock fights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:20.720
taken directly from earlier, better Godzilla&nbsp;
movies. It sold well as a late '60's kid movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.800
but it was also a sign of the dark&nbsp;
times ahead for the Godzilla franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.560
Stock monster fights aren't&nbsp;
awful on their own, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.320
so no one will blame you for watching&nbsp;
this oddly edited string of them.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.720
With "Ebirah, Horror of the Deep," we're moving&nbsp;
away from the truly disliked films and getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:44.720
into "mixed reviews" territory. The main drawback&nbsp;
of this film is how different it is than the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:51.120
traditional Godzilla movies — most notably by not&nbsp;
including much of Godzilla at all until the end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.960
The plot is pretty decent, though: a man loses his&nbsp;
brother at sea, learns from a mystic that he might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:04.080
still be alive, and heads out on a stolen sailboat&nbsp;
to find him — only to run into a nasty oversized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:09.360
lobster called Ebirah. Godzilla snoozes&nbsp;
for a while before finally doing his thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.160
defeating the crustacean by ripping its&nbsp;
claws off with an assist from Mothra.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.960
The effects and quality hadn't gotten&nbsp;
any better than the earliest films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.000
but that's one of the things&nbsp;
fans of "Ebirah" like about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.840
It's just the major lack of Godzilla&nbsp;
that really drives this movie down.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:32.880
"Godzilla: Tokyo S.O.S." is a bit confusing.&nbsp;
The plot involves Mothra showing up and telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.680
everybody they need to get the original&nbsp;
Godzilla's bones out of Mechagodzilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.760
and lay them to rest because it's disrespectful to&nbsp;
use a dead lizard's bones to raise a new monster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.120
Then, Godzilla shows up — not the original&nbsp;
Godzilla, who is now inside Mechagodzilla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:53.920
but a second, newer Godzilla — and if that's&nbsp;
not weird enough, Mothra ends up helping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:59.200
Mechagodzilla for some reason instead of going&nbsp;
through with dismantling the mechanical beast.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.920
"Tokyo S.O.S." definitely wasn't as bad&nbsp;
as some of the films that preceded it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:07.920
but it's generally considered to be&nbsp;
a lazy attempt by the producers of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.040
the Millennium films to keep up&nbsp;
with the old rate of production.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.280
"Terror of Mechagodzilla" was the last&nbsp;
Godzilla film in what many consider the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.480
tail end of a declining franchise — at&nbsp;
least before its resurrection in 1984.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.200
By this point, Godzilla movies had&nbsp;
largely moved away from horror fare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:32.080
and into the realm of bizarre creations seemingly&nbsp;
conjured from the mind of a six-year-old.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:37.520
"Terror of Mechagodzilla" bombed at the&nbsp;
box office, but it has its defenders.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:42.320
Most of the love for this movie can be traced&nbsp;
to the return of veteran director Ishirō Honda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.480
the man behind the original 1954&nbsp;
"Godzilla" and several of its sequels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:52.320
Honda didn't save the movie entirely, but he did&nbsp;
manage to give it the feel of a Godzilla flick of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:58.080
decades past. "Terror of Mechagodzilla"&nbsp;
was Honda's final directorial effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.840
and far from his best contribution to&nbsp;
the franchise, but it was clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:05.840
his passion for the monster had never&nbsp;
faded, even after twenty-one years.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:11.520
If Rotten Tomatoes' scoring system is to&nbsp;
be trusted, then "Godzilla: Final Wars"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:17.600
is as mediocre as a movie can be, weighing in at&nbsp;
a perfect 50 percent. It's part of the Millennium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:23.360
era, in which overarching narrative was largely&nbsp;
abandoned in favor of pure spectacle and bizarre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:28.800
twists. "Final Wars" was certainly unique,&nbsp;
but that wasn't necessarily a good thing.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.120
The film might be hit or miss, but it&nbsp;
sure is fun. A trove of classic monsters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.120
and aliens descends on the Earth,&nbsp;
leaving Godzilla to save the day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:40.640
Classic stylings with modern&nbsp;
effects bring the action to life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:45.280
but the acting of the human characters is bad&nbsp;
enough to ruin any suspension of disbelief.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:52.400
"Godzilla Against Mechagodzilla" is another mostly&nbsp;
unremarkable film from the Millenium era, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:57.360
distinguishes itself by being the first movie in&nbsp;
the franchise in which women are truly essential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:03.120
to the narrative. Kaiju aren't just for boys,&nbsp;
after all. The film also had a more progressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:08.080
and diverse cast of characters, which the&nbsp;
franchise had been trending toward since the '90s.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.480
The Mechagodzilla suit featured in&nbsp;
this film is polarizing among fans:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:17.280
some think the newer design is a worthy evolution,&nbsp;
while others seem to think that it should've kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.960
its classic look. Either way you see it, you'll&nbsp;
probably agree the plot is on the weird side:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:27.840
the old Godzilla is dead, a new Godzilla&nbsp;
has risen to take the former's place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.240
and now the old Godzilla's bones are&nbsp;
used to create the Mechagodzilla cyborg.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:38.080
At least it's still easier to follow than&nbsp;
its direct sequel, "Godzilla: Tokyo S.O.S."

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:45.520
"Godzilla vs. Megalon" may not have wowed critics&nbsp;
past or present, but fans certainly seem to prefer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:50.160
it over most of the films of the Millennium&nbsp;
era. The film is still a "versus" title from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:56.400
the downturned Shōwa era of the franchise, but&nbsp;
it was better than most at the time, even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:02.080
it was being aggressively marketed toward children&nbsp;
in a desperate bid to keep the rubber suit filled.

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:05.440
Although franchise revenues were&nbsp;
continually dwindling at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:11.200
"Godzilla vs. Megalon" made relatively decent&nbsp;
money at the box office. Much of its success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:16.320
was thanks to efficient advertising involving&nbsp;
the new character Jet Jaguar, whose design was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:22.480
the result of a fan contest that Toho held the&nbsp;
previous year. Thankfully, "Godzilla vs. Megalon"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:27.440
actually feels like a Godzilla film, and for&nbsp;
that, it's earned itself a place at number 25.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:32.400
In a confusing twist of fate, some&nbsp;
people were actually disappointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:37.280
that "Godzilla 2000" wasn't a direct&nbsp;
sequel to the 1998 American version.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:41.520
For the majority of fans, however, it&nbsp;
was a breath of fresh air in comparison.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:45.920
"Godzilla 2000" had many of the&nbsp;
stylings of the older films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:50.000
giving viewers the version of the King of&nbsp;
Monsters they feared had been lost to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:55.280
new world of computer-generated imaging — even&nbsp;
though there's still plenty of bad CGI to behold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:02.000
The story is also off: for some reason, "Godzilla&nbsp;
2000" was disconnected from all previous Godzilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:07.360
films and raised enough questions about continuity&nbsp;
to leave anyone familiar with the series with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:11.920
lingering sense of confusion. All in all,&nbsp;
though, it was a decent movie that blended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:17.360
modern animatronics with a suit we were pleased to&nbsp;
see hadn't yet made it to the metaphorical trash.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:24.000
Mechagodzilla has most recently been seen&nbsp;
stopping Godzilla from destroying cities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.560
but he wasn't always written that&nbsp;
way. In "Godzilla vs. Mechagodzilla,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:33.440
the robotic titan's first appearance in&nbsp;
the franchise, Mechagodzilla is a robot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:39.520
sent by aliens to destroy Japan. Naturally,&nbsp;
it's up to Godzilla proper to save the day.

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:45.600
"Godzilla vs. Mechagodzilla" gave us one of&nbsp;
Godzilla's most iconic foes, and for that, fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:51.600
can't thank it enough. We’re also gifted with the&nbsp;
“so bad it’s good” guardian monster King Caesar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.960
That being said, it wasn't the best movie&nbsp;
it could've been: the film suffers from some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:03.040
pacing issues and features some truly bizarre&nbsp;
musical choices. It's also gorier than any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:08.000
the previous entries in the franchise, which&nbsp;
is a plus for some and a minus for others.

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:15.120
When it comes to "Godzilla vs. SpaceGodzilla,"&nbsp;
most fans have one of two opinions:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:19.280
Either it's one of the best Godzilla&nbsp;
films they've ever seen, or it's barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:24.960
worth watching. If you're into Godzilla because&nbsp;
you like watching big lizards destroy things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:29.760
then you'll probably enjoy this movie; after&nbsp;
all, you get two Godzillas for the price of one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:36.080
SpaceGodzilla is believed to be a clone of the&nbsp;
original lizard, but with several enhancements:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:42.480
most notably, those massive crystal spikes growing&nbsp;
out his back. There's some fun tension between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:47.280
SpaceGodzilla and Godzilla Junior,&nbsp;
too, reminiscent of an abandoned child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:51.360
taking out his parental qualms on the&nbsp;
sibling who got all the attention.

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:56.400
Outside of that, though, the movie isn't very&nbsp;
good. The psychic subplot continues from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.520
previous films, but it doesn't have much impact&nbsp;
on anything that actually happens. The special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:06.160
effects are also lacking, not quite on par&nbsp;
with the other Heisei [HEY-say] era films.
 
 

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:11.600
"Godzilla vs. Mechagodzilla II"&nbsp;
scored pretty high with critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:15.440
ending up with an impressive Rotten&nbsp;
Tomatoes score of 83 percent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.480
Not all fans would agree with that assessment,&nbsp;
however: the website's audience ranking is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:25.600
hovering just above 70 percent, which feels&nbsp;
more in line with the "fun" factor of this film.

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:31.360
Mechagodzilla is built from the remains&nbsp;
of Mecha-King Ghidorah and, this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:38.240
is meant to be humanity's savior. The movie&nbsp;
also introduces Godzilla Junior — not Minilla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:42.800
the baby-faced creature of the past, but&nbsp;
a younger version of Godzilla that grows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:48.160
up to be half as big, yet just as terrifying. The&nbsp;
only issue fans really seem to have with the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:52.720
is with the updated Mechagodzilla design&nbsp;
that lacks some of the classic elements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:55.920
such as the iconic finger rockets.

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:01.600
While Mothra is often thrown into Godzilla movies&nbsp;
just because fans like the character, she doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:07.280
just show up for no reason in "Godzilla and&nbsp;
Mothra: The Battle for Earth." Here, Mothra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:13.120
takes center stage as the benevolent protector&nbsp;
of Earth. Godzilla rampages, Battra falls from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:18.960
the sky to teach the world a lesson, and Mothra&nbsp;
joins in the fight to stop Godzilla, eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:24.160
sealing him under the sea. Then, Mothra takes off&nbsp;
to tour the universe for some reason or another.

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:28.400
The effects in this movie are pretty great:&nbsp;
Mothra looks cooler than ever before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:33.040
with bigger wings and better coloring. There&nbsp;
really aren't too many negatives about this movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:37.840
but there aren't many highlights, either. It's a&nbsp;
good Godzilla film, but nothing groundbreaking.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:43.600
The battle between King Kong and&nbsp;
Godzilla, in whatever form it might take,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:49.040
is probably the most iconic of all the kaiju&nbsp;
fights in the genre. The warm-blooded primate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:54.640
versus the sea-dwelling reptile is a matchup that&nbsp;
can't be missed. It's so popular, in fact, that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.640 --> 00:13:00.160
remake was released in 2021 — and it's also the&nbsp;
reason this movie made it this far on the list.

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:06.320
Unfortunately, 1962's "King Kong vs. Godzilla"&nbsp;
is considered a bit of a blemish on the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:11.200
among hardcore fans; not because it wasn't a&nbsp;
good movie, but because it's a symbol of when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:16.560
Godzilla transitioned from existentially&nbsp;
terrifying to silly and family-friendly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:21.440
If you haven't yet seen the modern reboot, you&nbsp;
should definitely pop this in the VCR first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:25.520
and witness the original battle between&nbsp;
the world's two most beloved monsters.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:30.880
Critics weren't too pleased with&nbsp;
Godzilla's most recent solo outing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:36.400
"Godzilla: King of the Monsters." Many cite the&nbsp;
plot mechanics as "tricky" and hard to follow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:40.000
while others claimed the storytelling&nbsp;
was worthless and couldn't be saved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:43.360
However, many fans would be quick to disagree.

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:49.200
Sure, the plot and story are sub-optimal, but&nbsp;
the action and spectacular effects drove audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:54.800
reviews far into the realm of "favorable." There's&nbsp;
no man in a rubber suit, so it doesn't offer much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.800 --> 00:14:00.640
in the way of cheesy, classic appeal, but it's&nbsp;
undeniably an entertaining two hours. "Godzilla:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:05.120
King of the Monsters" features plenty&nbsp;
of our favorite titans, such as Mothra,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:10.400
Rodan, and the magnificent three-headed menace&nbsp;
King Ghidorah, all looking more lifelike than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:15.840
ever. It's a testament to the lasting legacy&nbsp;
that Godzilla has cultivated over the years.

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:19.840
"Long live the king."

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:23.920
In an era where the Godzilla franchise&nbsp;
had moved toward younger audiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:28.640
it was rare to see an installment that&nbsp;
pandered to the adult crowd the way the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:33.200
early, horror-type entries did.&nbsp;
"Godzilla vs. Gigan" was pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:37.840
much the only Godzilla movie from the '70s&nbsp;
that adults could say was made for them.

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:42.160
The film starts out almost like "Jurassic&nbsp;
Park:" a group of business owners want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:47.440
summon the world's most fearsome monsters for&nbsp;
their own gain. As you'd assume, this doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:53.200
work out — but not in the way you'd think. The&nbsp;
businessmen are actually aliens trying to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:58.560
over Earth by calling on Gigan and King&nbsp;
Ghidorah to wreak havoc on the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:04.320
The film's highlight is a two-on-two fight with&nbsp;
Godzilla and Anguirus battling the two destroyers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:08.880
"Godzilla vs. Gigan" is definitely&nbsp;
worth watching if you have the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:12.240
but like the others up to this point,&nbsp;
it probably won't be your favorite.

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:18.000
One of the aspects of the Godzilla franchise&nbsp;
that made it such a beast over half a century ago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:23.360
is the social commentary with which it packs its&nbsp;
best films. This started with the original and has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:28.480
been present on occasion ever since. Often,&nbsp;
those that break this precedent are simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:33.280
action for action's sake with no other redeeming&nbsp;
qualities, made solely to entertain and nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:37.440
else. "Godzilla vs. Hedorah," [HEY-dore-ah&nbsp;
or hey-DORE-uh] however, has plenty to say.

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:40.800
The plot revolves around the&nbsp;
pollution that plagues the Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:44.720
which makes it the perfect target&nbsp;
for the smog-sucking monster to drop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:50.160
in from space and muck around. Fortunately&nbsp;
for everyone else, Godzilla saves the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:55.360
and the world receives a grim reminder of the&nbsp;
consequences of their less-than-green practices.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:02.720
The film commonly referred to as "Invasion of&nbsp;
Astro-Monster" has gone by three different names,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.720 --> 00:16:08.720
depending on date and location. When you see&nbsp;
someone mention "Godzilla vs. Astro-Monster''&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:14.320
or "Godzilla vs. Monster Zero," they're talking&nbsp;
about the same movie. Other than that minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:19.600
confusion, which really doesn't factor into&nbsp;
the quality of the feature, it's pretty good.

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:23.760
Since the film is a direct sequel to&nbsp;
"Ghidorah, the Three-Heared Monster,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:29.120
the fan-favorite character comes back for a second&nbsp;
helping. The movie is enjoyable, but not quite as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:34.480
good as the first; either of these Ghidorah movies&nbsp;
could've done without the other since they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:40.640
basically the same story. Regardless of whatever&nbsp;
name you call it, "Invasion of Astro-Monster"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:46.000
is perfectly watchable, even if it doesn't&nbsp;
quite match up to Ghidorah's previous outing.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:50.080
In order to really appreciate the&nbsp;
Heisei era of the Godzilla franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:54.400
and take in all the fun the '80s&nbsp;
and '90s entries have to offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:58.240
it's best to view them as a similar,&nbsp;
yet separate string of films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:04.080
If you do, you'll likely enjoy "Godzilla vs.&nbsp;
Biollante," since it's the epitome of this age.

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:08.880
Some of Godzilla's cells have been recovered from&nbsp;
a volcano, and different groups have different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:14.160
ideas for what to do with them. Naturally, this&nbsp;
causes a fight, and the cells ultimately land in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:19.360
the hands of the Japanese military, where they are&nbsp;
spliced with rose DNA and mutate into the creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.360 --> 00:17:26.240
known as Biollante. Naturally, you can guess who&nbsp;
Biollante ends up in a big kaiju battle against.

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:31.360
"Godzilla vs. Biollante" has plot,&nbsp;
action, and of course, rubber suits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:34.880
making it a darn good film as far&nbsp;
as Godzilla himself is concerned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:40.320
As for the acting and character development?&nbsp;
They're far from perfect, to put it kindly.

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:46.160
The first Godzilla movie of the 2020s&nbsp;
is certainly a film worth seeing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:49.840
with some of the best special&nbsp;
effects and CGI that money can buy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:55.120
Some purists might not enjoy the film's sleek&nbsp;
Hollywood look, but it's undeniable that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.120 --> 00:17:59.840
"Godzilla vs. Kong" features some of the most&nbsp;
impressive visuals the franchise has to offer.

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:05.520
Unfortunately, the movie falls a little flat&nbsp;
when it comes to character development and drama.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:10.800
Even though Kong seems to change sizes depending&nbsp;
on what is happening in the film, the exciting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:15.840
action and visual direction overshadow any&nbsp;
narrative flaws. If you want to see the world's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:21.520
two most famous kaiju duke it out in stunning&nbsp;
4K resolution, this is the movie to watch.

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:27.760
King Ghidorah makes any Godzilla movie&nbsp;
worth watching, even if said films aren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:33.360
cinematic masterpieces — when it comes to&nbsp;
fun, monster-y goodness, King Ghidorah's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:39.200
three heads are hard to beat. As such, "Godzilla&nbsp;
vs. King Ghidorah" is a delightfully cheesy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:44.640
B-movie that exists purely to entertain —&nbsp;
especially if you watch the English dub.

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:48.880
"It's a dinosaur! A gigantic&nbsp;
dinosaur is attacking our boys!"

00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:56.080
Despite a bizarre time travel storyline, "Godzilla&nbsp;
vs. King Ghidorah" succeeds through delightful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.080 --> 00:19:01.440
special effects and epic monster designs.&nbsp;
There's two boss fights for the price of one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:06.240
as Godzilla's fight against Ghidorah is&nbsp;
followed up with an epic battle against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:11.760
Mecha-King Ghidorah, an enhanced version of the&nbsp;
monster teleported in from the 23rd century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:17.280
This is the perfect Godzilla film for a midnight&nbsp;
movie with a couple friends and lots of popcorn.

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:23.520
Some superfans may see this particular film&nbsp;
as problematic within the Godzilla canon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:29.360
but it's an important piece of the franchise's&nbsp;
history. "Son of Godzilla" is the first time we,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:36.000
well, meet the son of Godzilla. This reptilian&nbsp;
child, a clumsy troublemaker called Minilla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:40.800
is a walking embodiment of the lighthearted&nbsp;
humor the series was shifting toward at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:47.280
In this first appearance, Minilla has to help&nbsp;
Godzilla defeat a giant spider called Kumonga.

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:53.600
Many view the inclusion of Minilla as an even&nbsp;
further push in a childish direction — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.600 --> 00:19:58.560
it honestly was — but that doesn't make&nbsp;
"Son of Godzilla'' any less of a classic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.560 --> 00:20:03.360
With the franchise having been built on small sets&nbsp;
with big rubber suits from the very beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:05.840
the classics are what it's all about, after all.

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:12.960
"Destroy All Monsters" is generally considered the&nbsp;
last good Godzilla film before the franchise went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:19.600
into its '70s slump. The movie is exceedingly&nbsp;
fun, if not chaotic, and packs a heavy punch:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:23.120
it has the largest brawl out of any Godzilla film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.120 --> 00:20:27.440
bringing in eleven of the classic creatures to&nbsp;
duke it out. Not all of these monsters get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:32.560
luxury of full screen time, with some only&nbsp;
having short cameos, but that doesn't stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:37.600
the film's final battle from feeling like&nbsp;
a tokusatsu version of "Avengers: Endgame."

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:41.760
Viewers have to wade through the&nbsp;
movie's slow start to get to the action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:46.640
but every ounce of patience is worth the wait.&nbsp;
It's the last remnant of "the good old days"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:51.920
going out with a bang. You'll definitely want to&nbsp;
watch this particular film with subtitles, though:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:54.640
the English dubbing is pretty&nbsp;
awful and hard to follow.

00:20:55.920 --> 00:21:01.760
Other than the name being an absolute&nbsp;
mouthful, "Godzilla, Mothra and King Ghidorah:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:07.600
Giant Monsters All-Out Attack" is an all-around&nbsp;
fantastic film and is easily the best of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:13.120
Millennium era. Fortunately, this movie doesn't&nbsp;
feel like an early 21st century garbage heap like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:18.160
most of the others from the time: instead, we get&nbsp;
the man in the green rubber suit and everything!

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:23.200
Godzilla is back with a vengeance, infused&nbsp;
with the restless spirits of World War II&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:28.560
veterans and ready to destroy Japan in&nbsp;
retaliation for its crimes — that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:33.120
until three powerful guardians come&nbsp;
to the country's aid: Baragon, Mothra,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:38.480
and King Ghidorah. Even without these fan&nbsp;
favorites on screen, the movie's spirit is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:44.320
the political commentary of Godzilla's past,&nbsp;
and for that, it weighs in at number nine.

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:49.520
The top ten is highly competitive, and&nbsp;
"Godzilla vs. Destoroyah" is no lightweight:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:54.800
in fact, it's the only Godzilla movie to score&nbsp;
a perfect 100 percent on Rotten Tomatoes. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.800 --> 00:22:00.320
movie was the finale of the Heisei series, and&nbsp;
brought the era to a banging halt. Godzilla was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:05.440
built up over the course of a decade so he could&nbsp;
meet his end in the final moments of this film.

00:22:05.440 --> 00:22:10.480
The finale to the Heisei era was originally&nbsp;
planned to involve Godzilla dueling with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:15.920
ghost of the 1954 Godzilla, which definitely&nbsp;
would've been a showdown to remember.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.920 --> 00:22:21.040
Instead, we got Godzilla having a literal&nbsp;
nuclear meltdown while looking like he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:26.640
needs the world's largest Tums. That's not&nbsp;
to say the film isn't absolutely fantastic:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.640 --> 00:22:29.280
it is, and it's definitely worth checking out.

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:35.920
The year was 1984, and the Godzilla franchise had&nbsp;
been sitting near death on a nine-year hiatus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:40.720
The goofiness of the Shōwa era&nbsp;
had put a lot of people off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:45.760
and if the big guy was going to grace the screen&nbsp;
once more, the film would have to be exceptional.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:52.560
Sure enough, it was. The nuclear lizard rose from&nbsp;
the sea once again in "The Return of Godzilla."

00:22:52.560 --> 00:22:57.600
The first film of the Heisei era brought the&nbsp;
world's favorite kaiju back to its roots. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.600 --> 00:23:01.920
plot made heavy commentary on the Cold War&nbsp;
tensions that had been weighing the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:08.000
down for years leading up to the film, returning&nbsp;
Godzilla to a mature, less childlike format. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:11.840
everyone was as happy as they should've&nbsp;
been to see the big man back, however;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:16.080
the franchise had laid dormant for years,&nbsp;
and there were some cynics who believed it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:20.880
should've stayed that way. Most Godzilla&nbsp;
fans — particularly those in Japan — were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:25.680
more than happy to see the radioactive menace&nbsp;
make his grand return to the silver screen.

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:31.520
There's no denying "Ghidorah, the Three-Headed&nbsp;
Monster" has flaws when it comes to production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:36.640
quality, and its stature is more a&nbsp;
product of nostalgia than anything else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:42.800
So why is it so high on this list? Well, no other&nbsp;
movie in the franchise introduced King Ghidorah!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:47.760
The three-headed creature is one of the most&nbsp;
powerful and awesome monsters in the franchise —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:52.720
not just a city destroyer, but a potentially&nbsp;
world-ending threat — and for that alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.720 --> 00:23:58.480
this movie shines. That's not to say the film&nbsp;
doesn't have other premium qualities, of course.

00:23:58.480 --> 00:24:02.160
As opposed to the more human-like&nbsp;
beasts they'd come to expect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:07.200
Ghidorah gave fans a truly monstrous&nbsp;
monster. As for the human characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:12.640
they are surprisingly well developed and help&nbsp;
weave humor effortlessly throughout the tension.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.640 --> 00:24:17.840
"Ghidorah, the Three-Headed Monster" earned its&nbsp;
spot at number six, and it wasn't given lightly.

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:24.000
"Godzilla Raids Again" was the first Godzilla&nbsp;
sequel, released just one year after the original.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:29.520
It proved to be a stunning film in its own&nbsp;
right, with a ferocity in its choreography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:34.880
that other kaiju pictures of the day lacked. This&nbsp;
was the first Godzilla film to pit the king of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:41.120
monsters against another creature of its size:&nbsp;
specifically, the irradiated dinosaur Anguirus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:45.520
Ultimately, the spiked creature isn't&nbsp;
powerful enough to take Godzilla down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.520 --> 00:24:48.080
leaving it up to the humans&nbsp;
to save the day once again.

00:24:48.640 --> 00:24:54.160
For all of its high points, "Godzilla Raids&nbsp;
Again" has its faults when it comes to plot:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.160 --> 00:24:58.480
most notably, its erasure of Godzilla's&nbsp;
death at the end of the first movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:02.880
Regardless, its importance to the growth of&nbsp;
the franchise truly can't be understated.

00:25:03.760 --> 00:25:08.480
Despite what some purists might say,&nbsp;
entertainment is just as important as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:15.040
historical and intellectual value when it comes to&nbsp;
ranking kaiju movies, and the 2014 "Godzilla" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:20.560
just about as entertaining as they come. Nobody&nbsp;
expected an American Godzilla film with advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:20.560 --> 00:25:26.640
special effects to work — 1998 killed that idea&nbsp;
for us — but this movie showed us that one bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.640 --> 00:25:31.360
apple doesn't ruin a bunch of awesome giant&nbsp;
monsters beating the crap out of each other.

00:25:31.360 --> 00:25:34.400
The way this movie is shot has&nbsp;
a lot to do with its quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.960 --> 00:25:39.920
Instead of watching monsters rampage in&nbsp;
the distance, the 2014 film gave viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:44.560
a point of view that made us feel we were&nbsp;
right there, looking up at the carnage.

00:25:44.560 --> 00:25:45.280
"What are you doing?"

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:46.960
"I have to see this."

00:25:47.840 --> 00:25:53.120
The progression withholds details in just the&nbsp;
right amounts, showing us bits and pieces until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.120 --> 00:25:58.080
we're burdened by the crushing weight of&nbsp;
a sci-fi horror. The movie was masterfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.080 --> 00:26:02.880
done in just about every way — we just wish&nbsp;
we could've seen a little more Bryan Cranston.

00:26:03.920 --> 00:26:10.080
The best Godzilla movie of the 21st century,&nbsp;
"Shin Godzilla" is not just one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:15.680
entertaining movies about the giant lizard,&nbsp;
but also a biting piece of political satire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.680 --> 00:26:20.160
In this movie, which takes place in an&nbsp;
entirely separate continuity from every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:26.320
other Godzilla film, not only is Godzilla upset&nbsp;
about being woken up from his decades-long nap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:31.200
but he's also punishing Japan in&nbsp;
retaliation for the Fukushima disaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.200 --> 00:26:35.280
and humanity's generally&nbsp;
irresponsible use of nuclear power.

00:26:35.280 --> 00:26:40.800
The movie spends a lot of time inside bureaucratic&nbsp;
meetings in which the Japanese government tries to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:46.960
manage the crisis, but that never detracts at all&nbsp;
from the film's power. "Shin Godzilla" embodies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:52.400
the soul of Godzilla that seemed to have been lost&nbsp;
in the Millennium era's need for mindless action.

00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:57.440
Much of "Shin Godzilla's" success can&nbsp;
be attributed to the masterful direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.440 --> 00:27:03.840
of Hideaki Anno, the creator of the iconic&nbsp;
anime series "Neon Genesis Evangelion,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:08.400
who presents a disturbing version of&nbsp;
Godzilla unlike any other put to screen.

00:27:09.360 --> 00:27:15.600
Even though Mothra made her first appearance&nbsp;
in the 1961 film of the same name, "Mothra vs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:22.000
Godzilla" marked the first of many times the giant&nbsp;
insect would face the giant lizard in battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:27.680
"Mothra vs. Godzilla" is generally considered the&nbsp;
greatest Godzilla installment from its decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:30.880
not to mention the second&nbsp;
best in the entire series.

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:35.200
Comedic overtones mesh with a serious&nbsp;
storyline to bring us the underdog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:40.240
fight of a lifetime. Godzilla is&nbsp;
a powerhouse bent on raising hell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.240 --> 00:27:45.840
while Mothra is forced to dig deep and use more&nbsp;
strength than ever before to save Japan from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:52.240
destructive rampage. It's a classic story of&nbsp;
overcoming evil, but with two giant monsters and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.240 --> 00:27:57.600
all the cutting-edge effects the early '60s could&nbsp;
muster. The human storyline isn't quite as good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.600 --> 00:28:01.920
but that's something Godzilla fans&nbsp;
have come to expect over the years.

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:07.120
You're probably not surprised to see the&nbsp;
first-ever Godzilla movie at the top of the list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:13.040
and why would you be? Ishirō Honda's "Godzilla"&nbsp;
is the film that sparked more than 65 years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.040 --> 00:28:19.200
sequels, reboots, and series based on the King&nbsp;
of Monsters. Regardless of its follow-ups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:24.640
however, the very first "Godzilla" was a&nbsp;
powerful piece raised high on its own merit.

00:28:24.640 --> 00:28:30.240
Movie critic Roger Ebert called "Godzilla,"&nbsp;
quote, "the Fahrenheit 9/11 of its time,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:36.880
and for good reason. "Godzilla" was created&nbsp;
as a response to American radiation testing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:42.880
Pacific waters, as well as the lasting effects&nbsp;
of the nuclear bombs dropped on Hiroshima and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:50.160
Nagasaki in 1945. The movie was a warning about&nbsp;
nuclear technology in the hands of human beings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:55.840
and the destructive force that such a power could&nbsp;
have if used incorrectly. Sure, the movie can look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:55.840 --> 00:29:02.880
a bit silly at times, as many sci-fi movies from&nbsp;
the '50s do, but its concept is utterly brilliant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:08.400
setting a precedent for other Godzilla stories&nbsp;
to speak out about human folly in all its forms.

